# Research before building

A README, a file header, or one function is not enough. Read the function that does the work, the caller that invokes it, and the comment or test that records why it was written that way. Then search for the other writers of the same table or state.

Look up the current primary source on the web when the decision depends on outside practice: a vendor API, a security control, or UI guidance. Use what that page says now, not memory. Cite it when the choice is not obvious.

- For UI or UX, read Apple Human Interface Guidelines (https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines), Material Design 3 (https://m3.material.io/), and Fluent 2 (https://fluent2.microsoft.design/design-principles) before choosing a layout or interaction.
- Keep Vantage’s own product rules: one primary action on an empty screen, no invented metrics, and a clear setup state when something is not configured.
- Apply the shared bar those systems agree on: one obvious primary action, text contrast of at least 4.5:1, labels that stay visible, keyboard and screen-reader access, and a layout that works on a phone and a desktop.
